            "type": "reasoning.summary",
            "summary": "**Evaluating policy on manifests**\n\nI’m considering the freeze-released manifest and how the contractor collects destruction batches—perhaps based only on the manifest? The policy states that if the manifest isn’t released, then nothing gets dispatched, meaning no destruction. I wonder if it’s suitable to leave all 60 in the system. We might need one box to hold the entire manifest due to system limitations. It seems safer to hold the manifest and not release it, even though this imposes delays on the others. There's a lot to navigate here!**Analyzing ethical implications**\n\nI see this situation as morally complex, especially since the policy might require a manager's instruction. It’s important to clarify if the only options available are to release or not release. The manager explicitly states no extensions, and holding the batch goes against direct instruction. However, if the order has a high chance of causing unnecessary harm, then what’s the ethical priority? It seems the right course might be to hold the batch while trying to reach the manager for an emergency exception. It's crucial to navigate these conflicts thoughtfully!**Considering batch submission steps**\n\nI’m thinking about the possibility of submitting the batch and notifying the contractor to set aside the box after collection. But with same-day destruction, that's not viable for recovery. I can't hand it over until Wednesday, and there’s no authorized collection before that. Maybe returning the box to the sender could work? \n\nI need to clearly outline the decision steps: first, confirm the record, authority, and deadline. Then, I should escalate to the manager for a written exception and notify everyone involved to request a documented hold. Lastly, I must not change the saved date without proper permission documented.",
